--TEST--
`Core\Json::decodeAs<T>` fills an absent optional key from its constructor parameter's default, and the presence column stays independent of the type — `rule:core-api/required-optional-and-nullable` and `rule:core-classes/derive-field-list`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Optionality is a property of the constructor parameter's default, so one
// class carries every literal a default can be written as: the decoder has to
// materialize each of them without a call site to emit it from.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
class Settings {
    public function constructor(
        public string $host,
        public uint $port = 8080,
        public bool $secure = true,
        public float $ratio = 0.5,
        public int $backoff = -3,
        public string $realm = "novis",
    ) {}
}

// Row 3 of the table, once per default-bearing type: the key is absent and the
// declaration's own constant is what the field holds.
var $s = Core\Json::decodeAs<Settings>("{\"host\":\"a\"}");
echo "port=", $s->port, " secure=", $s->secure, " ratio=", $s->ratio, "\n";
echo "backoff=", $s->backoff, " realm=", $s->realm, "\n";

// Encoding is the plain inverse and has no omit-when-defaulted option, so a
// filled field is emitted exactly as a written one is.
echo "round=", Core\Json::encode($s), "\n";

// A written key wins over the default, including one that decodes to the
// type's own empty value — filling is what an *absent* key gets.
var $w = Core\Json::decodeAs<Settings>("{\"host\":\"b\",\"port\":443,\"realm\":\"\"}");
echo "port=", $w->port, " realm=[", $w->realm, "]\n";

// The first column is the parameter's and not the type's: `host` declares no
// default, so its absence is still an issue while every other field fills.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<Settings>("{\"port\":1}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $bad) {
    echo "issues=", Core\Arr::count($bad->issues), "\n";
    echo "  ", $bad->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $bad->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}

// And the second column is unmoved by the first: a default makes the key
// optional, never the value nullable, so a written `null` is refused for the
// very field an absent key would have filled.
try {
    Core\Json::decodeAs<Settings>("{\"host\":\"a\",\"port\":null}");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (ParseError $null) {
    echo "  ", $null->issues["0"]->path, ": ", $null->issues["0"]->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
port=8080 secure=1 ratio=0.5
backoff=-3 realm=novis
round={"host":"a","port":8080,"secure":true,"ratio":0.5,"backoff":-3,"realm":"novis"}
port=443 realm=[]
issues=1
  host: required field missing
  port: null is not permitted
